1989: ## 27. The full cap fractional period and the remaining annular attachment
1990: 
1991: On the actual E annulus \(v=0,\ 0<|T|<\epsilon\), the
1992: presently executed collision atlas has fixed nodes
1993: \(j\pmod N\). The period \(h\) has zero \(v\)-order;
1994: \(g\) shifts \(j\) by \(N\). The source and Cartier
1995: units on this annulus have zero \(v\)-order. Thus
1996: positive \(T\) continuation fixes all \(N\) labels.
1997: In the cap atlas \(A=v^{kn}r_0^nA_0\) shifts \(j\)
1998: by \(kn\) while rotating the root. Its fixed-component
1999: permutation is \(j\mapsto j+kn\pmod N\), with \(k\)
2000: cycles of length \(m\) when \(n=\pm1\). An equivariant
2001: central overlap would identify these component local
2002: systems. The recorded atlases therefore still require
2003: the full changed-origin deck. This identifies the
2004: attachment gap; it does not rule out another filling.
2005: 
2006: The source part of that deck can be calculated exactly.
2007: At \(v=0\), \(G_0=(0,0)\) is two-torsion on every
2008: smooth source fibre. Its marked Tate representative
2009: satisfies
2010: \[
2011:                          P^2Q=1.                           \tag{SZ.68}
2012:  \]
2013: Indeed \(2G_0=O\) gives \(P^2=Q^j\) for a locally
2014: constant integer \(j\); the retained asymptotics
2015: \(P\sim16/T,\ Q\sim T^2/256\) give \(j=-1\).
2016: The already proved chord identity \(U_1^2=T^{-1}\)
2017: has in the theta gauge source multiplier \(P^{-2}=Q\)
2018: and line multiplier \(c_X^{-2}\). Dividing by the
2019: literal source period \((Q,P)\) gives
2020: \[
2021:                          c_X^2=T/P.                        \tag{SZ.69}
2022:  \]
2023: The exact central periods are consequently
2024: \[
2025:  h=(Q,P^a),\qquad
2026:  g=(P^{-b},\,2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda P).                         \tag{SZ.70}
2027:  \]
2028: For \(a=2\) the central Cartier line is trivial because
2029: the restricted horizontal divisors \(2G\) and \(O\)
2030: coincide. Its Tate trivialization is \(ZV\), since
2031: \(P^2=Q^{-1}\). In this frame the reference suspension
2032: over translation \(-G\) has scalar \(+2\). In the
2033: good \(a=1,b=2\) branch it is the scalar \(-2\).
2034: These constants also follow directly from the Hom map:
2035: \[
2036:  F_{21}=T/(2v)+O(1),\quad F_{12}=-T/(2v)+O(1),\qquad
2037:  q_1/(vF_{21})\to2,\quad q_1/(vF_{12})\to-2.                \tag{SZ.71}
2038:  \]
2039: 
2040: Put \(\mathcal B=QP^2\), with central value one on
2041: the E annulus. In the good \(m=4p\) branch the
2042: identity division section has representative
2043: \[
2044:                          R=\mathcal B^{1/m},               \tag{SZ.72}
2045:  \]
2046: using the root tending to one. Its \(m\)-th power
2047: represents \(2G\), with the retained \(Q\) period.
2048: It equals the actual cap branch: both specialize
2049: to the identity, and the local inverse of
2050: multiplication by \(m\) is unique there.
2051: This identity continues along the connected
2052: smooth annulus.
2053: 
2054: In the reduced \(m=4p-1\) branch, \(R-G\) is
2055: \(m\)-torsion at \(v=0\) and lies in the small
2056: identity inverse branch at III. It is therefore
2057: zero on that annulus. The actual representative is
2058: \[
2059:                 R=P\,\mathcal B^{-(m-1)/(2m)}.              \tag{SZ.73}
2060:  \]
2061: Its \(m\)-th power is \(P Q^{-(m-1)/2}\), the
2062: representative of \(G\) with its exact integral
2063: period. This retains the period in (SZ.54).
2064: 
2065: On a chosen root sheet the actual theta/Cartier
2066: cap map is
2067: \[
2068:  \begin{array}{c|c|c}
2069:  \text{branch}& A\text{ in the }(h,g)\text{ periods}
2070:                     &\text{line multiplier}\\ \hline
2071:  a=1,\ b=2,\ m=4p&
2072:  h^{-n/m}g^{n/m}&(g_V/P)^{n/m}\\
2073:  a=2,\ b=1,\ m=4p-1&
2074:  h^{\,n(m-1)/(2m)}g^{n/m}&
2075:                        (P^{m-1}g_V)^{n/m}.
2076:  \end{array}                                                \tag{SZ.74}
2077:  \]
2078: The specified constant finite character is retained.
2079: The source multiplier in each row is translation
2080: by \(-nR\). Its \(m\)-fold line norm agrees with
2081: the constructed cap lift, since the extra \(h\)
2082: exponent is an integral source-period identification.
2083: The ratio of the line lifts initially is a base
2084: unit with cyclic norm one. Retain its prescribed
2085: central character \(\chi\), and put
2086: \(f=\log(r/\chi)\) on the actual cap-root polydisc.
2087: The norm gives \(\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}f(D^jz)=0\).
2088: The explicit extending finite average
2089: \[
2090:  \psi(z)=\frac1m\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}j f(D^jz)
2091:  \quad\text{satisfies}\quad
2092:                   \psi(Dz)-\psi(z)=f(z).
2093: \]
2094: Conjugating by the actual scalar gauge
2095: \(\exp(-\psi)\) removes this norm-one unit.
2096: This gauge commutes with the suspension and
2097: retains the physical delta circle; it changes
2098: the annular overlap by its literal extending
2099: unit. After this carried gauge the remaining
2100: ratio is the constant character \(\chi\),
2101: fixed by the prescribed cap marking. One
2102: does not replace cyclic norm one by an
2103: \(m\)-th-power equation before this conjugacy.
2104: This verifies the actual line map.
2105: 
2106: The central scalars are
2107: \((-2\lambda)^{n/m}\) in the good branch and
2108: \((2\lambda)^{n/m}\) in the reduced frame \(ZV\).
2109: The norms retain their full source periods:
2110: \[
2111:  A^m=h^{-n}g^n\quad\text{(good)},\qquad
2112:  A^m=h^{\,n(m-1)/2}g^n\quad\text{(reduced)}.                 \tag{SZ.75}
2113:  \]
2114: Thus the inverse cap map has the full fractional
2115: period
2116: \[
2117:  f_{\rm cap}=
2118:  \begin{cases}
2119:  (n/m,-n/m),&a=1,\ b=2,\\
2120:  (-n(m-1)/(2m),-n/m),&a=2,\ b=1 .
2121:  \end{cases}                                                \tag{SZ.76}
2122:  \]
2123: Its order modulo the period lattice is exactly
2124: \(m\) for \(n=\pm1\). The scalar-only E deck has
2125: fractional period
2126: \[
2127:                     f_{\rm sc}=(bn/(2m),-n/m).             \tag{SZ.77}
2128:  \]
2129: In the reduced branch their difference is
2130: \((-n/2,0)\), retaining the actual half source
2131: period. Direct multiplication gives
2132: \[
2133:  E f_{\rm cap}=-nk\,\delta,\qquad
2134:  H(f_{\rm cap}-f_{\rm sc})=
2135:  \begin{cases}
2136:  (0,0),&a=1,\\
2137:  (-n,n),&a=2 .
2138:  \end{cases}                                                \tag{SZ.78}
2139:  \]
2140: The reduced vector is the source/Cartier valuation
2141: of \(B_0^{-n}\), including its chamber change.
2142: 
2143: The executable
2144: \(\texttt{source\_zero\_cap\_fractional\_period.py}\)
2145: checks source representatives, integral norms,
2146: both signs, central Cartier constants and the
2147: full matrices for 576 admitted numerical rows.
2148: The displayed formulas establish the same
2149: identities for every positive \(p,k\).
2150: 
2151: The maps (SZ.74) are chosen-sheet maps.
2152: Continuation of \(\mathcal B^{1/m}\) around
2153: \(q_1=0\) changes the division section by an
2154: actual \(m\)-torsion point. The successive
2155: root-sheet deck and its cyclic norm must be
2156: constructed before using this as a finite
2157: action on the collision filling. The abstract
2158: finite order of (SZ.76) does not itself identify
2159: the protected noncentral cusp quotient or its
2160: multiplicity. That exact root-sheet, fan and
2161: Cartier arrow remains the next attachment step.
2162: 
2163: ### 27.1. Successive root sheets and the protected cusp
2164: 
2165: The successive-sheet norm is recorded independently in
2166: the blind-control note
2167: \(\texttt{notes/part3\_source\_zero\_cyclic\_norm.md}\)
2168: and its exact certificate. For a positive strict
2169: \(q_1\) loop, \(\mathcal B\) and the line radicands
2170: in (SZ.74) have winding one. Thus the continued
2171: good maps satisfy
2172: \[
2173:        A_j/A_0=(\zeta_m^{-jn},\zeta_m^{jn})
2174:                     \quad\text{on }(Z,V).
2175:  \]
2176: For \(m=4p,\ n=\pm1\), their cyclic product has
2177: the residual map \((Z,V)\mapsto(-Z,-V)\), followed
2178: by \(h^{-n}g^n\). This is an actual two-torsion
2179: source and line map. The reduced ratios are
2180: \[
2181:        A_j/A_0=
2182:        (\zeta_m^{jn(m-1)/2},\zeta_m^{jn}),
2183:  \]
2184: whose cyclic product has no residual for odd \(m\).
2185: The strict \(q_2\) loop gives the inverse sheet
2186: increments and the same good order-two residual.
2187: A loop enclosing both nodes has
2188: \(\operatorname{wind}\mathcal B=0\). These calculations
2189: retain the distinction between the strict-node
2190: continuations and the E annulus by their exact
2191: maps; no finite action is inferred from them.
2192: 
2193: There is also an exact obstruction to one proposed
2194: root quotient. On the natural \(q_1=\xi^m\) cover,
2195: the protected I period lattice is \(m\mathbb Z^2\).
2196: Its components are labelled by
2197: \(\mathbb Z^2/m\mathbb Z^2\). The translation
2198: \(mf_{\rm cap}\) is \((n,-n)\) in the good branch
2199: and \((-n(m-1)/2,-n)\) in the reduced branch.
2200: Its second coordinate is a unit modulo \(m\),
2201: so every component orbit has length \(m\).
2202: The extra good map \((-Z,-V)\), when retained,
2203: also moves a generic torus point; it supplies
2204: no divisorial inertia. Thus \(q_1\) still has
2205: order \(m\) at a generic descended divisor.
2206: The prescribed noncentral cusp has order one.
2207: This root-translation quotient therefore does
2208: not furnish the required attachment. The
2209: obstruction concerns this actual proposed
2210: quotient, and does not assign a global
2211: nonexistence result.
2212: 
